# Capture debug view 9 (raw surface normal as RGB) to test whether sprite INTERIORS carry
# any relief at all.
#
# PREREQUISITE: a THROWAWAY clone of an explored save, --var WORLD=<name>.
#
# Why this view answers the question definitively: `surface_normal()` in sprite.frag.hlsl is
# a 4-tap gradient of ALPHA (`dx = sR.a - sL.a`), so wherever alpha is saturated -- the
# whole interior of a wall or building sprite -- dx = dy = 0 and the normal is exactly
# (0,0,1). Mode 9 encodes the normal as n*0.5+0.5, so a flat normal is rgb(128,128,255),
# a flat lavender. If the interiors read that colour with near-zero variance, no amount of
# nrm_relief / nrm_amount tuning can produce face shading, because those knobs multiply a
# gradient that is zero. That is the difference between "needs tuning" and "needs the
# distance-transform normal atlas" (GRAVEYARD_KEEPER_VISUAL_PLAN section 3b).
#
# 9 F7 presses reach mode 9, gated on the log so a dropped key fails the run rather than
# silently handing back a capture of some other mode.
launch --dont-debugmsg --world ${WORLD}
waitlog "opened document .*sidebar_hud.rml" timeout=300000
focus
settle timeout=25000
shot warmup avg=4
wait 6000
settle timeout=15000

rect play 0.25,0.12,0.55,0.80

shot mode0 avg=8

key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 400
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
wait 250
key "{F7}"
waitlog "lighting debug mode 9" timeout=20000
wait 2500
shot normals avg=6
stats normals in=play

log
stop
